[01:48] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The Insta360 Ace Pro 2 screen displaying "Timelapse Mode" with a landscape preview.
AUDIO: 'Hello everybody my name is Aki. In this video, I\'m going to show you how to capture stunning low light images using the Insta360 Ace Pro 2. Let\'s get started! First up, timelapse. The Ace Pro 2 lets you create amazing low light timelapses with just a few simple tweaks.'

[02:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You\'ve got two options, Timelapse Mode or Interval Mode. Timelapse Mode is the quickest way to get results. The camera automatically creates a Timelapse video for you, so you don\'t need to worry about post editing. It\'s perfect for sharing straight to social media.'

[02:14] [scene:result]
VISUAL: A timelapse video of a traditional Japanese building at night.
AUDIO: 'But if you after the best image quality, go with Interval Mode. This gives you a full control over the settings and lets you capture high quality raw photos for editing later. The best exposure settings will depend on the lighting condition, so here are the settings I\'ve used as a reference.'

[02:28] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: On-screen display of camera settings (Auto/M, ISO, EV, WB) with WB set to 6400K.
AUDIO: 'For daytime shots, ISO 100, white balance at 5600K and a shutter speed of 1/1000. During sunset or sunrise, keep ISO at 100, white balance at about 6400K and set the shutter speed auto since the brightness constantly changes.'

[02:43] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: On-screen display of camera settings for Starlapse, showing ISO 800 and WB 4000K.
AUDIO: 'And a nice city scenes like this, set ISO to 100, white balance to about 4600K and a shutter speed of 5 seconds to get the cool motion blur effect from moving objects, like cars and airplanes. For starlapse, use ISO 800 to 2000 white balance at 4000K'

[02:56] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Adobe Lightroom interface with a cityscape photo, showing synchronization settings.
AUDIO: 'and a 30 second shutter speed to capture the stars. And no matter the situation, always shoot in PureShot+RAW for a maximum editing flexibility. Once you\'re done shooting, it\'s time to edit.'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'First, upload your photos into Lightroom for color grading and adjustments. After that, export them as JPEG images then bring them into Premium Pro as a sequence. Render the timelapse and you\'re all set. Check out the final result.'

[03:22] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The Insta360 Ace Pro 2 screen displaying "PureVideo" mode selection.
AUDIO: 'Next, let\'s talk about PureVideo Mode. The Ace Pro 2 is fantastic for low light video capture. To use this feature, just select PureVideo Mode and the camera automatically adjust the exposure for you. All you need to do is set the white balance.'

[03:35] [scene:result]
VISUAL: A video of fireworks exploding in the night sky.
AUDIO: 'A quick tip, even though the camera performs great in low light, it\'s still best to shoot in places with plenty of light, like a city at night. If you\'re capturing bright objects like fireworks, remember to lower the EV to around -2 or -3.'

[03:47] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: The Insta360 Ace Pro 2 screen showing "PureShot+RAW" format selection.
AUDIO: 'This prevents the camera from over boosting the ISO keeping your dark skies sharp and noise free. Finally, let\'s talk about photo timelapse. This technique requires a little more effort than a normal hyperlapse but the results are worth it.'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'First, switch to Photo Mode and select PureShot+RAW. At this location I set ISO 100, shutter speed 1/15 and white balance at 4800K. Feel free to adjust the settings based on your environment. So here\'s the process.'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Pick a reference point. Something like a building street light or anything that catches your eye. Next, turn on the Grid feature on your camera. This helps you keep the object centered in every shot. Once you\'ve got it centered, snap your photo.'

[04:28] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A person taking a photo on a busy city street at night.
AUDIO: 'Now take a step forward and repeat. The trick here is to position the camera in the same place for every shot. Don\'t rush, just keep a steady pace as you walk. Once you\'ve captured the shots, color correct the raw photos in Lightroom and import the JPEG as a sequence into After Effects.'

[04:43] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Adobe After Effects interface with a night cityscape, showing motion tracking controls.
AUDIO: 'Now to stabilize the footage, select Stabilize Motion and check the Rotation box. Choose two reference points, one clear point as track point one and another directly in line with it as track point 2.'

[04:55] [scene:result]
VISUAL: A hyperlapse video of a city street at night, showing smooth motion and light trails.
AUDIO: 'Click play to let After Effect do its thing. If it doesn\'t look right, you may need to manually adjust the points. When it\'s done, click apply and choose X and Y for stabilization. Lastly, scale the image to fit the frame and you\'ve got an amazing hyperlapse.'

[05:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'For an extra touch, add some Radio Blur and it will look even more dynamic. That\'s it for today\'s video! I hope you enjoyed it and I\'ll see you guys very soon in the next one.'
